module gunbc.spark.managed_grant_install


// INSTALLING THE GRANT THE RECONCILE FOUND MISSING, which is the step that turns a receipt naming a
// gap into a process that closes it. Until this existed the only way a grant reached a host was an
// operator at a keyboard -- out-of-band actuation, manual application not committed as source, and
// the reason the reboot leg had no remedy but to ask a human.
//
// SUDOERS IS VALIDATED BEFORE IT IS INSTALLED, not after, and this ordering is the whole safety
// argument. A malformed drop-in file in /etc/sudoers.d does not fail that file alone: sudo refuses
// to parse the entire sudoers set and every privileged operation on the host stops, including the
// one that would repair it. So `visudo -c -f` runs against the STAGED copy, and the file reaches
// /etc/sudoers.d only after sudo itself has said it parses. A validation that ran afterwards would
// be reporting the damage rather than preventing it.
//
// The file lands via `install` rather than `mv` because sudo refuses to read a drop-in that is
// group- or world-writable, and ownership and mode are then facts of the command that placed it
// rather than of whatever umask the staging write happened to run under.

// THE ORDER IS THE RECORD'S SHAPE, and each phase carries its OWN type. An earlier cut froze the
// four phases as an ordered List<PlannedRemoteStep> and asserted, with a forward scan over step
// identities, that validation preceded installation -- a question about a list the executor did not
// run, since the credential cutover left it consuming only the staged write and building its own
// privileged commands. The scan dissolved with that list.
//
// Naming the phases as fields was then claimed to make the bad order unrepresentable. It did not,
// and the overclaim is recorded here rather than quietly corrected: with validate and install BOTH
// typed AdmittedRootArgv, a construction holding them the wrong way round typechecked and would
// have installed an unvalidated sudoers file. The fields fixed their own order while leaving the
// values swappable. Giving each phase a distinct carrier is the actual wall -- the swap is a type
// error now -- and the ordering witness stays as the control that the two carriers do not collapse
// back into one.
// THE UNPRIVILEGED PHASES CARRY THEIR OWN FACTS, not a pre-built step. An intermediate revision
// wrapped each in a distinct name around the SAME generic PlannedRemoteStep, which stopped a field
// swap and nothing else: StageGrantFile { step: cleanup_step } was still constructible, so the wrong
// operation could still be run in the stage position by rewrapping rather than by swapping. A
// nominal tag around an already-realized step is a label, not a wall. Each carrier now holds what
// only that phase can mean -- a typed write, or the two paths to remove -- and the PlannedRemoteStep
// is DERIVED at the execution boundary.
//
// RUNG, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: this does NOT reach structural impossibility, and the reason
// is a checker behaviour rather than a modeling choice. Swapping the two phase FIELDS is refused --
// `expected std.constructors.Product(StageGrantFile), got std.constructors.Product(CleanupGrantStaging)`, located, nothing executed.
// But REWRAPPING is not: constructing StageGrantFile { write: <List<String>> } typechecks and runs,
// even though `write` is declared TypedRemoteFileWrite, a sole_constructor product of path and
// content. Measured 2026-08-20 by mutation, against a witness that forces the field through
// RemoteFileBytes { write: stage.write }. So named-product-against-named-product is enforced here
// and non-product-against-named-product is not, and a claim of "the wrong operation has no
// representation" would be inflation for the second case.
//
// Honest standing: phase ASSIGNMENT is structurally guaranteed against field swaps and
// mutation-verified against rewrapping. This is NOT a new error class and must not be named as one
// (§3 -- a second name for one concept). The corpus already carries the family: std.realization_schedule
// walk_finalization_note measured that return position and a data annotation are not checked at all
// while ARGUMENT position is checked and refuses correctly, and its own second correction is a
// record-field instance -- WalkPlan { finalization: NoWalkFinalization } typechecked because both
// arms inhabit one sum, repaired by PARAMETERIZING the carrier rather than by the checker; and the
// hollow-alias construction wall plan states the general form, that nominal-type discrimination is
// not enforced at these seams and the class is wrapper-payload conflation generally. What the
// measurement here ADDS to that family is one position not previously recorded: a record-literal
// FIELD holding a wholly unrelated non-product value -- neither a sibling arm of the declared sum
// nor a wrapper of its payload, so neither existing row covers it. Next-rung trigger is the family's,
// not a private one: field-position type enforcement in the v2 typechecker, at which point the rewrap
// mutation becomes a typecheck refusal and this note collapses to one line. Until then the local
// remedy is the one walk_finalization_note already proved -- carry the distinction in the type.
type StageGrantFile { write: gunbc.typed_remote_file_write.TypedRemoteFileWrite }
type CleanupGrantStaging { staged_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr, inner_staging_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

fn stage_grant_file_step(grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant, stage: StageGrantFile) -> gunbc.remote_operation.PlannedRemoteStep {
  gunbc.remote_operation.PlannedRemoteStep {
    identity: spark_grant_install_step_identity(grant: grant, phase: "stage"),
    operation: gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteFileBytes { write: stage.write, mode: gunbc.typed_remote_file_write.RemoteFileModeOwnerReadWrite },
  }
}

fn cleanup_grant_staging_argv(cleanup: CleanupGrantStaging) -> std.types.List<String> {
  ["rm", "-f", cleanup.staged_path as String, cleanup.inner_staging_path as String]
}

fn cleanup_grant_staging_step(
  grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant,
  cleanup: CleanupGrantStaging,
) -> gunbc.remote_operation.PlannedRemoteStep {
  gunbc.remote_operation.PlannedRemoteStep {
    identity: spark_grant_install_step_identity(grant: grant, phase: "cleanup"),
    operation: gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteArgv { argv: cleanup_grant_staging_argv(cleanup: cleanup) },
  }
}

type SparkGrantInstallPlan
  = SparkGrantInstallSteps {
      grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant
      stage: StageGrantFile
      validate: gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.GrantSudoersValidation
      install: gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.GrantSudoersInstallation
      cleanup: CleanupGrantStaging
    }
  | SparkGrantInstallRefused { grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant, cause: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

// STAGED IN THE INSTALLER'S OWN HOME, not the grantee's. The first cut of this took the executor
// home as a constant, so a run connecting as the operator tried to write into the automation
// account's directory and got Permission denied at the far side -- a failure that reads as a broken
// installer rather than as the wrong account being named. Whose home it is follows from who is doing
// the writing, which is exactly the fact the transport principal already carries.
fn spark_grant_staging_path(grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant, principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr) -> FilePath {
  join([
    spark_managed_principal_home(login: principal) as String, "/.gunbc-staged-sudoers-",
    spark_managed_grant_dropin_name(grant: grant) as String,
  ], "") as FilePath
}

fn spark_grant_install_step_identity(grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant, phase: String) -> std.types.NonEmptyStr {
  join([phase, "-", spark_managed_grant_dropin_name(grant: grant) as String], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr
}

fn spark_grant_admit_argv(identity: std.types.NonEmptyStr, raws: std.types.List<String>) -> gunbc.remote_operation.PlannedRemoteStep? {
  match portable_remote_words(raws: raws) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: words } => Present {
      value: gunbc.remote_operation.PlannedRemoteStep {
        identity: identity,
        operation: gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteArgv { argv: portable_remote_words_strings(words: words) },
      },
    }
  }
}

fn spark_grant_install_plan(grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant, principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr) -> SparkGrantInstallPlan {
  let staged = spark_grant_staging_path(grant: grant, principal: principal)
  match typed_remote_file_write_seal(
    path: staged,
    content: spark_managed_grant_sudoers_content(grant: grant),
    allowed_scope: gunbc.file_access.FileTreeScope { root_path: spark_managed_principal_home(login: principal) },
  ) {
    gunbc.typed_remote_file_write.TypedRemoteFileWriteAdmissionRefused { cause: why } =>
      SparkGrantInstallRefused { grant: grant, cause: why }
    gunbc.typed_remote_file_write.TypedRemoteFileWriteAdmitted { write: w } => {
      let cleanup_phase = CleanupGrantStaging {
        staged_path: staged as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
        inner_staging_path: typed_remote_file_staging_path(write: w) as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
      }
      match spark_grant_admit_argv(
        identity: spark_grant_install_step_identity(grant: grant, phase: "cleanup"),
        raws: cleanup_grant_staging_argv(cleanup: cleanup_phase),
      ) {
        Absent =>
          SparkGrantInstallRefused {
            grant: grant,
            cause: "the staging cleanup argv is not portable under the far-side re-parse" as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
          }
        Present { value: _admitted } =>
          SparkGrantInstallSteps {
            grant: grant,
            stage: StageGrantFile { write: w },
            validate: gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.GrantSudoersValidation { staged_path: staged as std.types.NonEmptyStr },
            install: gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.GrantSudoersInstallation {
              staged_path: staged as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
              dropin_path: spark_managed_grant_dropin_path(grant: grant),
            },
            cleanup: cleanup_phase,
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE PHASES THE EXECUTOR ACTUALLY RUNS, in the order it runs them, read off the same fields it
// consumes. This is a receipt projection and not a schedule: nothing downstream may reorder it,
// because there is nothing here to reorder.
fn spark_grant_install_operation_labels(plan: SparkGrantInstallPlan) -> std.types.List<String> {
  match plan {
    SparkGrantInstallRefused { grant: _, cause: _ } => []
    SparkGrantInstallSteps {
      grant: g2, stage: st, validate: v, install: i, cleanup: c,
    } =>
      [
        stage_grant_file_step(grant: g2, stage: st).identity as String,
        admitted_root_argv_label(command: grant_sudoers_validation_argv(command: v)),
        admitted_root_argv_label(command: grant_sudoers_installation_argv(command: i)),
        cleanup_grant_staging_step(grant: g2, cleanup: c).identity as String,
      ]
  }
}

// A SINGLE-STEP SEQUENCE REDUCED TO ITS STANDING. The sequencer answers in lists because it runs
// lists; cleanup is one step, and an empty result means it was never attempted rather than that it
// succeeded quietly -- so the identity-bearing unattempted arm is the init, never an empty list.
fn spark_grant_cleanup_standing(
  identity: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  standings: std.types.List<gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepStanding>,
) -> gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepStanding {
  fold(standings, init: gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepUnattempted { identity: identity }, f: fn(_, s) { s })
}

fn spark_grant_install_plan_wire(plan: SparkGrantInstallPlan) -> String {
  match plan {
    SparkGrantInstallRefused { grant: g, cause: c } =>
      join([
        "  grant-install grant=", spark_managed_grant_wire(grant: g) as String,
        " REFUSED cause=", c as String, "\n",
      ], "")
    SparkGrantInstallSteps { grant: g, stage: _, validate: _, install: _, cleanup: _ } =>
      join([
        "  grant-install grant=", spark_managed_grant_wire(grant: g) as String,
        " steps=", join(spark_grant_install_operation_labels(plan: plan), ","),
        "\n",
      ], "")
  }
}

// ── Actuation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE PRINCIPAL IS A PARAMETER OF THE PLAN, not a constant of this module, because installing a
// grant is exactly the operation the grantee cannot perform for itself. The automation account holds
// enable-linger and nothing else, so it can neither write /etc/sudoers.d nor run visudo; a bootstrap
// that assumed otherwise would fail with a password demand and no statement of who could have done
// it instead. Naming the transport principal makes "who installs privilege" a modeled fact rather
// than an assumption buried in a target constructor.
fn spark_grant_install_transport(
  context: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.FleetSshExecutionContext,
  host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity,
  principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> gunbc.host_effect.HostEffectTransport {
  gunbc.host_effect.FleetSsh {
    target: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.SshTarget {
      endpoint: fleet_probe_endpoint_for(host: host as String) as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
      principal: principal,
    },
    context: context,
  }
}

// AWAITING A CREDENTIAL IS NOT A FLOOR, and calling it one was the error this arm is named for. An
// earlier revision called this BlockedOnRootOfTrust and rendered a sudoers recipe for a human to
// apply -- reasoning that privilege cannot be self-granted, therefore the mutation must be manual.
// The first half is true and the second does not follow: software cannot manufacture root authority,
// but the input that resolves that is AUTHORIZATION, not MUTATION. Supplying a credential once and
// hand-authoring host state are different acts, and only the first is unavoidable.
//
// The cost of the confusion was real: the process asked an operator to reproduce a plan it had
// already frozen, on a host it could already reach, and the result would then be unobserved by the
// thing that needed it. This arm now means exactly "no credential was supplied", and its remedy is
// the credential the authorization model has specified all along.
// AN INSTALL THAT REPORTED SUCCESS IS NOT A HOST THAT HOLDS THE GRANT, and the gap between those two
// sentences is where this whole vertical started: a sudoers line applied by hand converged nothing
// the process could see. `install` exiting zero says a file was placed; whether sudo now grants the
// command is a separate fact that only sudo can state, and it can disagree -- a drop-in with the
// wrong account name, a syntactically valid rule for a path that does not exist, or a sudoers set
// whose earlier entry takes precedence all place a file successfully and grant nothing.
//
// So the install re-reads the enumeration through the grantee's own principal and requires the grant
// to appear. Not attempted when nothing was installed, because a readback of an install that never
// ran would report the pre-existing state as the result.
type SparkGrantReadback
  = GrantReadbackConfirmed
  | GrantReadbackDisagreed { standing: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | GrantReadbackNotAttempted

type SparkGrantInstallOutcome
  = SparkGrantInstallAwaitingOperatorCredential {
      host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity
      grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant
      principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr
    }
  | SparkGrantInstallExecuted {
      host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity
      grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant
      standings: std.types.List<gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepStanding>
      privileged: std.types.List<gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.PrivilegedStepOutcome>
      readback: SparkGrantReadback
      cleanup: gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepStanding
    }
  | SparkGrantInstallNotAttempted {
      host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity
      grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant
      cause: std.types.NonEmptyStr
    }

// THE SEQUENCE IS MIXED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT HOMOGENEOUS. Staging and cleanup are ordinary
// unprivileged operations; validation and installation are credential-bound. Running all four
// through one sequencer would have required a RemoteOperation that carries a credential, which is
// the generic { argv, stdin } carrier the ruling forbids -- it would reopen arbitrary script bodies
// through stdin under root. So the two privileged steps are their own operation with their own
// closed argv set, and the fold below halts across both kinds.
//
// The order is load-bearing and is now carried by the plan's SHAPE rather than asserted about it:
// validation runs against the STAGED copy before anything reaches /etc/sudoers.d, because a
// malformed drop-in there stops every privileged operation on the host including the one that would
// repair it. The staged file is removed after EVERY attempted staging -- including a failed one, and
// that is not pedantry: the staging step is itself a four-step typed convergence that writes
// <path>.gunbc-staging, modes it, renames it, then compares, so a refusal at mode or rename leaves
// the inner sibling behind and a refusal at compare leaves the final name. Cleanup therefore removes
// BOTH names, and an earlier cut that skipped cleanup whenever staging failed left exactly the files
// a failed staging is most likely to strand.
//
// Its standing is reported beside the install rather than folded into it: a failed rm leaves a 0600
// file in the installer's own home and is not a reason to call an installed grant uninstalled. Those
// are two facts and the receipt carries both, while the process exit requires both.
//
// RUNG, STATED HONESTLY: that cleanup runs after a FAILED staging is read off this function, not
// witnessed. spark_grant_install_execute needs a network transport, so no hermetic witness reaches
// the arm; what the witnesses do establish is that the cleanup command names BOTH staging paths.
// So the both-names half is mechanically preventable and the attempted-after-failure half sits at
// review diligence. Next-rung trigger: a transport seam a witness can drive with a refusing staging
// step, at which point the arm becomes executable evidence rather than a claim about this text.
// NO AUTHORIZATION, NO HOST MUTATION -- NOT EVEN A TRANSIENT ONE. The pending standing is answered
// before the first far-side operation, so the awaiting arm truthfully owes no cleanup: nothing was
// staged, so nothing can be stranded. An earlier cut staged first and then discarded the cleanup
// standing when it built that arm, while the cleanup predicate answered `true` for it
// unconditionally -- a receipt that could not show a cleanup failure it had already caused.
func spark_grant_install_execute(
  context: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.FleetSshExecutionContext,
  host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity,
  principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  administrator: gunbc.spark.bootstrap_credential.SparkCredentialStanding,
  grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant,
) -> SparkGrantInstallOutcome
  uses net: std.resources.Network
{
  let plan = spark_grant_install_plan(grant: grant, principal: principal)
  match plan {
    SparkGrantInstallRefused { grant: g, cause: c } =>
      SparkGrantInstallNotAttempted { host: host, grant: g, cause: c }
    SparkGrantInstallSteps {
      grant: g, stage: staged_step, validate: validate_cmd, install: install_cmd, cleanup: cleanup_step,
    } =>
      match administrator {
        gunbc.spark.bootstrap_credential.CredentialMaterializationPending =>
          SparkGrantInstallAwaitingOperatorCredential {
            host: host, grant: g, principal: principal,
          }
        gunbc.spark.bootstrap_credential.CredentialMaterialized { ref: _ } => {
          let lease = spark_bootstrap_credential_lease(
            host: host,
            administrator: administrator,
            grant: g,
            attempt_identity: spark_grant_install_ci_attempt_raw as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
          )
          let transport = spark_grant_install_transport(context: context, host: host, principal: principal)
          let cleanup_planned = cleanup_grant_staging_step(grant: g, cleanup: cleanup_step)
          let staged = spark_serving_run_step_sequence(
            steps: [stage_grant_file_step(grant: g, stage: staged_step)],
            transport: transport,
          )
          if spark_serving_step_sequence_failed(standings: staged) {
            SparkGrantInstallExecuted {
              host: host, grant: g, standings: staged, privileged: [],
              readback: GrantReadbackNotAttempted,
              cleanup: spark_grant_cleanup_standing(
                identity: cleanup_planned.identity,
                standings: spark_serving_run_step_sequence(steps: [cleanup_planned], transport: transport),
              ),
            }
          } else {
            {
              let validated = run_privileged_step(
                context: context, host: host, lease: lease,
                command: grant_sudoers_validation_argv(command: validate_cmd),
              )
              if privileged_step_succeeded(outcome: validated) == false {
                spark_grant_install_from_privileged(
                  host: host, grant: g, principal: principal, staged: staged,
                  outcomes: [validated], readback: GrantReadbackNotAttempted,
                  cleanup: spark_grant_cleanup_standing(
                    identity: cleanup_planned.identity,
                    standings: spark_serving_run_step_sequence(steps: [cleanup_planned], transport: transport),
                  ),
                )
              } else {
                {
                  let installed = run_privileged_step(
                    context: context, host: host, lease: lease,
                    command: grant_sudoers_installation_argv(command: install_cmd),
                  )
                  let readback = if privileged_step_succeeded(outcome: installed) {
                    spark_grant_read_back(context: context, host: host, grant: g)
                  } else {
                    GrantReadbackNotAttempted
                  }
                  spark_grant_install_from_privileged(
                    host: host, grant: g, principal: principal, staged: staged,
                    outcomes: [validated, installed], readback: readback,
                    cleanup: spark_grant_cleanup_standing(
                      identity: cleanup_planned.identity,
                      standings: spark_serving_run_step_sequence(steps: [cleanup_planned], transport: transport),
                    ),
                  )
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

// A CREDENTIAL THAT WAS NEVER ENROLLED IS NOT A FAILED INSTALL, and separating the two is what keeps
// "nobody has supplied authorization yet" from reading like "the installer is broken". Every other
// privileged failure stays an executed-and-failed install, because something was actually attempted.
//
// ONLY NotEnrolled TAKES THE AWAITING ARM. An earlier cut routed CredentialNotMaterialized here too,
// which rebuilt one layer up exactly the conflation the four-arm outcome exists to end: that arm
// carries neither the read cause nor the enrolled reference, so a missing environment variable, an
// unreadable file, an empty payload and a line-bearing payload all arrived at the reader as "no
// credential was supplied". They have different remedies and the first post-IAM run is precisely
// when telling them apart matters. NotMaterialized stays an EXECUTED install: staging and cleanup
// really did run against the host, and the privileged list carries its own located cause.
fn spark_grant_install_from_privileged(
  host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity,
  grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant,
  principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  staged: std.types.List<gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepStanding>,
  outcomes: std.types.List<gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.PrivilegedStepOutcome>,
  readback: SparkGrantReadback,
  cleanup: gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepStanding,
) -> SparkGrantInstallOutcome {
  if any(outcomes, o => match o {
    gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.CredentialNotEnrolled { lease: _ } => true
    gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.CredentialNotMaterialized { lease: _, cause: _ } => false
    gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.CredentialRejected { lease: _ } => false
    gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.PrivilegedCommandFailed { command: _, exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => false
    gunbc.spark.grant_privileged_operation.PrivilegedCommandSucceeded { command: _ } => false
  }) {
    SparkGrantInstallAwaitingOperatorCredential { host: host, grant: grant, principal: principal }
  } else {
    SparkGrantInstallExecuted {
      host: host, grant: grant, standings: staged, privileged: outcomes, readback: readback,
      cleanup: cleanup,
    }
  }
}

// THE READBACK IS THE GRANTEE'S OWN ENUMERATION, not the installer's. sudo answers "may THIS account
// run THIS command", and the account that matters is the one the serving lane will use -- asking as
// the installing principal would confirm a grant for the wrong subject entirely.
func spark_grant_read_back(
  context: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.FleetSshExecutionContext,
  host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity,
  grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant,
) -> SparkGrantReadback
  uses net: std.resources.Network
{
  let outcome = spark_grant_probe_outcome(context: context, host: host)
  match spark_managed_grant_standing_from_outcome(grant: grant, outcome: outcome) {
    gunbc.spark.managed_grant_reconcile.GrantHeld { grant: _ } => GrantReadbackConfirmed
    gunbc.spark.managed_grant_reconcile.GrantNotHeld { grant: _ } =>
      GrantReadbackDisagreed {
        standing: "the install reported success and the grantee's own sudo enumeration does not name the command" as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
      }
    gunbc.spark.managed_grant_reconcile.GrantStandingUnobserved { grant: _, cause: c } =>
      GrantReadbackDisagreed {
        standing: join(["the grant could not be re-observed after installing it: ", c as String], "") as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
      }
  }
}

// A SEQUENCE THAT HALTED IS NOT AN INSTALL. The phases are stage, validate, install and cleanup; a
// halt in the first three leaves the drop-in absent, and reporting that as installed would put a
// grant in the receipt that the host does not hold -- the exact confusion the reconcile exists to
// end. Cleanup is deliberately absent from this verdict: it runs after the fact that decides it, so
// consulting it would let a failed `rm` unsay a grant the host demonstrably holds.
// Convergence is re-established by the next observation, never by this exit code.
fn spark_grant_install_succeeded(outcome: SparkGrantInstallOutcome) -> Bool {
  match outcome {
    SparkGrantInstallAwaitingOperatorCredential { host: _, grant: _, principal: _ } => false
    SparkGrantInstallNotAttempted { host: _, grant: _, cause: _ } => false
    SparkGrantInstallExecuted { host: _, grant: _, standings: st, privileged: pr, readback: rb, cleanup: _ } =>
      spark_serving_step_sequence_failed(standings: st) == false
        && pr.length() > 0
        && fold(pr, init: true, f: fn(acc, o) { acc && privileged_step_succeeded(outcome: o) })
        && match rb {
             GrantReadbackConfirmed => true
             GrantReadbackDisagreed { standing: _ } => false
             GrantReadbackNotAttempted => false
           }
  }
}

// WHAT THE PROCESS NEEDS, NOT WHAT A HUMAN SHOULD TYPE. This function used to render the sudoers
// line, its mode and its path as instructions to apply by hand -- and that was the defect, not the
// wording. The unavoidable external input at this seam is AUTHORIZATION; the MUTATION is the
// process's job, and telling an operator to reproduce a frozen plan by hand both asks them to do
// work convergence owns and leaves the result unobserved by the thing that needed it.
//
// So the remedy named here is the credential the authorization model has always specified:
// OperatorBootstrapCredentialRequired, whose own secret_purpose says one-time, through the modeled
// secret boundary, not retained, not a serving requirement. The operator answers "may this exact
// grant be installed on this host", and supplies a credential. They never author host state.
fn spark_grant_credential_requirement(host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity, grant: gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkManagedGrant) -> String {
  let l = spark_bootstrap_credential_lease(
    host: host,
    administrator: gunbc.spark.bootstrap_credential.CredentialMaterializationPending,
    grant: grant,
    attempt_identity: spark_grant_install_ci_attempt_raw as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  )
  join([
    "REQUIRED: an administrator credential for ", l.bootstrap_principal as String,
    " on ", l.host as String,
    ", enrolled at ", secret_ref_version_resource(ref: spark_administrator_password_secret_ref),
    " (that name is supplied, never derived; this line states WHICH secret the run needs, and never",
    " that any run holds it)",
    ". Authorized purpose: ", l.authorized_purpose as String,
    ". The credential is materialized only for this step, delivered over stdin, and never enters an",
    " argv, a plan artifact, a workflow input or this receipt. Supplying it is the whole of the",
    " external input -- the mutation itself stays inside convergence and is read back independently.",
  ], "")
}

fn spark_grant_install_outcome_wire(outcome: SparkGrantInstallOutcome) -> String {
  match outcome {
    SparkGrantInstallAwaitingOperatorCredential { host: h, grant: g, principal: p } =>
      join([
        "grant-install host=", h as String,
        " grant=", spark_managed_grant_wire(grant: g) as String,
        " AWAITING-OPERATOR-CREDENTIAL installer=", p as String,
        " -- no credential was supplied, so the privileged step could not be attempted\n    ",
        spark_grant_credential_requirement(host: h, grant: g), "\n",
      ], "")
    SparkGrantInstallNotAttempted { host: h, grant: g, cause: c } =>
      join([
        "grant-install host=", h as String,
        " grant=", spark_managed_grant_wire(grant: g) as String,
        " NOT-ATTEMPTED cause=", c as String, "\n",
      ], "")
    SparkGrantInstallExecuted { host: h, grant: g, standings: st, privileged: pr, readback: rb, cleanup: cl } =>
      join([
        "grant-install host=", h as String,
        " grant=", spark_managed_grant_wire(grant: g) as String,
        " steps=", spark_serving_step_sequence_wire(standings: st),
        match spark_grant_install_first_failure_cause(standings: st) {
          Absent => ""
          Present { value: c } => join([" cause=", c], "")
        },
        "\n",
        join(map(pr, o => privileged_step_outcome_wire(outcome: o)), ""),
        match rb {
          GrantReadbackConfirmed => "    grant-readback CONFIRMED by the grantee's own sudo enumeration\n"
          GrantReadbackDisagreed { standing: why } =>
            join(["    grant-readback DISAGREED ", why as String, "\n"], "")
          GrantReadbackNotAttempted => "    grant-readback not attempted: nothing was installed\n"
        },
        "    staging-cleanup ", remote_step_standing_wire(standing: cl), "\n",
      ], "")
  }
}

fn spark_grant_install_first_failure_cause(standings: std.types.List<gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepStanding>) -> String? {
  fold(standings, init: none, f: fn(acc, st) {
    match acc {
      Present { value: _ } => acc
      Absent => remote_step_failure_cause(standing: st)
    }
  })
}

// ── The entry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

data spark_grant_install_receipt_path: String = "target/spark-grant-install-receipt.txt"

data spark_grant_install_ci_attempt_raw: String = "spark-grant-install-ci"

// THE GAP IS MEASURED BEFORE IT IS CLOSED, on the same run, and only the measured-missing grants are
// installed. Installing the whole desired population unconditionally would be cheaper to write and
// would be the absorbing fallback: it cannot tell a host that needs one grant from a host we could
// not read at all, and it would rewrite a correct drop-in on every run so no receipt could ever say
// nothing was needed.
type SparkGrantInstallRun {
  standing_report: String
  outcomes: std.types.List<SparkGrantInstallOutcome>
}

func spark_grant_install_host(
  context: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.FleetSshExecutionContext,
  host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity,
  principal: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  administrator: gunbc.spark.bootstrap_credential.SparkCredentialStanding,
) -> SparkGrantInstallRun
  uses net: std.resources.Network
{
  let outcome = spark_grant_probe_outcome(context: context, host: host)
  SparkGrantInstallRun {
    standing_report: spark_managed_grant_report(host: host, outcome: outcome),
    outcomes: map(spark_managed_grants_to_install(outcome: outcome), g =>
      spark_grant_install_execute(
        context: context, host: host, principal: principal,
        administrator: administrator, grant: g,
      )),
  }
}

fn spark_grant_install_run_text(run: SparkGrantInstallRun) -> String {
  join(concat(
    [run.standing_report],
    map(run.outcomes, o => spark_grant_install_outcome_wire(outcome: o)),
  ), "")
}

// THE VERDICT COMES FROM THE VARIANTS, never from the receipt text. This module's sibling records
// exactly this defect landing once already: an exit status decided by string_contains(body,
// "REFUSED") passed a run whose refusal arm spells "probe-refused" in lower case. Text is a
// projection of the outcomes; a projection cannot be the thing decided upon.
//
// An empty outcome list is SUCCESS here and only here: it means the reconcile found nothing missing,
// which the standing report states in its own words. The reconcile has already refused to put an
// unobserved grant in the install set, so "nothing to install" cannot arrive from ignorance.
fn spark_grant_install_run_succeeded(run: SparkGrantInstallRun) -> Bool {
  fold(run.outcomes, init: true, f: fn(acc, o) {
    acc && spark_grant_install_succeeded(outcome: o)
  })
}

// CLEANUP COMPLETION IS ITS OWN FACT, asked separately because it answers a different question than
// "does the host hold the grant". Folding it into the grant verdict would let a failed rm report a
// confirmed grant as absent; leaving it out of the EXIT entirely was the other error -- a confirmed
// grant with a stranded staging file exited green, the next reconcile saw no missing grant, and
// nothing ever came back for the file. So the receipt carries both and the exit requires both.
fn spark_grant_install_cleanup_completed(outcome: SparkGrantInstallOutcome) -> Bool {
  match outcome {
    SparkGrantInstallAwaitingOperatorCredential { host: _, grant: _, principal: _ } => true
    SparkGrantInstallNotAttempted { host: _, grant: _, cause: _ } => true
    SparkGrantInstallExecuted { host: _, grant: _, standings: _, privileged: _, readback: _, cleanup: c } =>
      match c {
        gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepCompleted { identity: _, execution: _ } => true
        gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepFailed { identity: _, execution: _ } => false
        gunbc.remote_operation.RemoteStepUnattempted { identity: _ } => false
      }
  }
}

fn spark_grant_install_run_cleanup_completed(run: SparkGrantInstallRun) -> Bool {
  fold(run.outcomes, init: true, f: fn(acc, o) {
    acc && spark_grant_install_cleanup_completed(outcome: o)
  })
}

// The grant reading, taken through the transport that will do the installing rather than a separate
// one: a standing measured as one principal and acted on as another is two facts wearing one name.
func spark_grant_probe_outcome(
  context: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.FleetSshExecutionContext,
  host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity,
) -> gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.SparkGrantOutcome
  uses net: std.resources.Network
{
  match run_typed_argv_transport(
    argv: ["sudo", "-n", "-l"],
    transport: spark_grant_install_transport(
      context: context,
      host: host,
      principal: spark_managed_executor_login as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
    ),
  ) {
    gunbc.typed_argv_exec.TypedArgvExecRefused { reason: why } => gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.GrantProbeRefused { cause: why }
    gunbc.typed_argv_exec.TypedArgvExecConverged { result: r } =>
      if r.success {
        gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap.GrantEnumerated { listing: r.stdout }
      } else {
        GrantAbsent { detail: join(["exit=", to_string(r.exit_code), " stderr=", trim(s: r.stderr)], "") }
      }
  }
}

// THE INSTALL NAMES ITS TARGET OR IT DOES NOT RUN, the same rule the reboot probe applies to itself
// and for the same reason: writing sudoers across a whole fleet from an unset variable is a fan-out
// nobody asked for, and the observe entry's all-hosts arm exists for reading, not writing.
func spark_grant_install_ci_wet() -> std.process.ProcessExit
  uses net: std.resources.Network
{
  match prepare_fleet_ssh_agent_context(attempt_raw: spark_grant_install_ci_attempt_raw) {
    gunbc.fleet_ssh_locus.FleetSshContextRefused { cause: cause } => exit_failure(reason: cause)
    FleetSshContextReady { context: context, receipt: context_receipt } =>
      match spark_converge_target_selection(execute_apply: true) {
        gunbc.fleet_multi_principal_probe.SparkConvergeTargetRefused { reason: why } => exit_failure(reason: why)
        gunbc.fleet_multi_principal_probe.SparkConvergeTargetAllObserveOnly =>
          exit_failure(
            reason: "spark_grant_install_ci_wet: SPARK_CONVERGE_TARGET names no host, and installing sudoers must never fan out across the fleet from an unset variable",
          )
        SparkConvergeTargetSingle { host: only } => {
          let now = clock_now_probed_at()
          let run = spark_grant_install_host(
            context: context,
            host: only,
            principal: spark_grant_install_principal(),
            administrator: spark_grant_install_administrator_standing(),
          )
          let body = join([
            "spark_grant_install_ci_wet probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, "\n",
            context_receipt,
            spark_grant_install_run_text(run: run),
          ], "")
          let w = Filesystem.Write(path: spark_grant_install_receipt_path, content: body)
          if w.success == false {
            exit_failure(reason: join(["grant install receipt write failed: ", w.error], ""))
          } else if spark_grant_install_run_succeeded(run: run) == false
              || spark_grant_install_run_cleanup_completed(run: run) == false {
            exit_failure(reason: body)
          } else {
            ExitSuccess
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

// WHICH PRINCIPAL INSTALLS PRIVILEGE, stated once. The automation account is the GRANTEE and cannot
// install its own sudoers entries, so the honest default is the operator account the bootstrap model
// already names as the transport for privileged effects. If that account cannot do it either, the
// run refuses with sudo's own diagnostic rather than leaving a human to guess who could.
fn spark_grant_install_principal() -> std.types.NonEmptyStr {
  spark_operator_bootstrap_login as std.types.NonEmptyStr
}

// THE STANDING NAMES THE ENROLLED REFERENCE, and that is ALL it names. Secret names are CARRIED,
// never derived (operator ruling 2026-08-07); the reference is spark_credential_workflow's authored
// row at its explicit version, whose own note says it sits on the serving-install critical path
// because "widening sudoers is itself a privileged effect that requires the administrator credential
// this lane resolves".
//
// IT IS NOT A REPORT ON THIS PROCESS'S FILE READ, and an intermediate revision made it one. That
// revision derived the standing from an observed materialization and looked stricter; it was a
// regression. Every unsuccessful read -- missing environment variable, unreadable file, empty
// payload, line-bearing payload -- collapsed into pending, pending became CredentialNotEnrolled, and
// that became "awaiting operator credential". So the located cause was destroyed one producer BEFORE
// the arm that exists to carry it, defeating that separation in the same commit that built it. The
// two questions are "is an exact credential enrolled" and "did this run receive its bytes", and
// answering the first with the second is the state-space conflation this outcome model is for.
//
// Naming the reference unconditionally is honest AT THIS ENTRY because the workflow step that
// precedes it does not reach a host at all unless the exact-version fetch returned 200: it exits on
// 403, on 404, on any other status, and on an empty payload, and writes the decoded exact bytes only
// on success. A run that bypasses that step reports "this run did not receive the enrolled
// credential" through CredentialNotMaterialized -- which is the truth -- rather than rewriting
// enrollment as pending.
fn spark_grant_install_administrator_standing() -> gunbc.spark.bootstrap_credential.SparkCredentialStanding {
  gunbc.spark.bootstrap_credential.CredentialMaterialized { ref: spark_administrator_password_secret_ref }
}
